#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 540.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130040} :activity/id #uuid "089b1802-0940-53e5-af98-b7a9c26bba1d" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048413 :topic/id #uuid "9cbfcac9-bcba-5bd9-9527-641435a44ebd" :topic/math-academy-id 1990} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130039 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129175 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128994 :question/id #uuid "7caffa88-776d-52e1-8b81-2ebf98aedebc" :question/math-academy-id "e-6122"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c0559f0c-a1ca-5ba8-a02e-71d584273958" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049574}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129421} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129855} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259568} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259569} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259570} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341844} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341885} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364900} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364907} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567015} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567043} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567086}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Double Integral Using Lower and Upper Riemann Sums"} :step/id #uuid "02381e3e-cb34-5f3b-8940-d1b55826683c" :step/math-academy-id 22783 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130120}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130040 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130379 :tutorial/content "Let $f(x,y)$ be a positive, continuous function defined on a closed and bounded rectangle $R,$ and let $P = P_1\\times P_2$ be a partition of $R.$ We know that the volume $V$ bounded by the surface $z=f(x,y)$ and the region $R$ in the $xy$-plane satisfies\n\n$$\nL(f,P) \\leq V \\leq U(f,P),\n$$\n\nwhere $L(f, P)$ and $U(f, P)$ are the lower and upper Riemann sums of $f$ with respect to $P.$\n\nImagine that our initial partition $P$ consists of a few rectangles only. Let's visualize the corresponding lower and upper Riemann sums, as shown below:\n\n![](images/89/89ad0fd33f34cfad4f55fc2085360878460df92479f5f938ecd254420457f3f4.png)\n\n \n\nThen, we refine $P$ by adding more points to $P_1$ and $P_2,$ resulting in more rectangles in $P{:}$ \n\n![](images/56/5635f58e19c47d6a9cfeab49c298ce5ca7aa1d8697e1b53390bb3bc31859637c.png)\n\n \n\nRefining the partition once again, we get the following:\n\n![](images/b2/b22f267ecf28627404eb90928e44d59b2873e88adc22ffaaa0a1a3335fffa307.png)\n\n \n\nAs we continue to refine $P$ by adding more and more points to $P_1$ and $P_2,$ we find that the values of the lower and upper Riemann sums approach the volume $V{:}$\n\n- $L(f, P)\\to V$ from below, and\n\n- $U(f, P) \\to V$ from above.\n\nWe define the **double integral** of $f(x,y)$ over $R$ as the *unique* number $I$ that satisfies the inequality\n\n$$\nL(f,P) \\leq I \\leq U(f,P)\n$$\n\nfor *all* partitions $P$ of $R,$ and we write\n\n$$\nI=\\iint\\limits_R f(x,y) \\, \\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe double integral gives the exact volume bounded between the surface $z=f(x,y)$ and $R.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "b9ba8fdf-255d-53c2-832a-34ed3374abb0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3884 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "7ef801e2-e7f6-542e-aab3-64391c418f88" :step/math-academy-id 22946 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130121}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130120 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129220 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129039 :question/id #uuid "3464909c-5b04-5cb1-96c9-a409fa105bb9" :question/math-academy-id "e-9494"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b9d2e98-8007-51ec-bfb6-cb9b48029a2a" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129853} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129854} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259571} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259572} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341883} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341884} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371679} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371686} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544806} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544863} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567097} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567105}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Double Integral Using the Mean Value of X and Y"} :step/id #uuid "5af32a1a-efb5-509d-a196-f411e5d46fc5" :step/math-academy-id 35550 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130122}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130121 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129221 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129040 :question/id #uuid "e75f2d8e-c165-5a24-942b-7326d7f11f0f" :question/math-academy-id "e-9497"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "89023510-57f4-52f9-90ed-eae07f48f707" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049348} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050883}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129422} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129423} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259547} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259548} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259549} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259550} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544824} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567009} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567059} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567098} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567101}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Definition of the Double Integral in Terms of Lower and Upper Riemann Sums"} :step/id #uuid "38972544-74f5-555d-aadd-8581d0da7887" :step/math-academy-id 35559 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130039}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130122 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130417 :tutorial/content "Throughout this lesson, we've defined the double integral of $f(x,y)$ over $R$ as the *unique* number $I$ (if it exists) that satisfies the inequality\n\n$$\nL(f,P) \\leq I \\leq U(f,P)\n$$\n\nfor *all* partitions $P$ of $R,$ and we write\n\n$$\nI=\\iint\\limits_R f(x,y) \\, \\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- Up to now, we've assumed that $f(x,y)$ is continuous and $R$ is closed and bounded. Whenever this is the case, a unique number $I$ will always exist.\n\n- For positive, continuous functions, the double integral gives the exact volume bounded between the surface $z=f(x,y)$ and $R.$\n\n- More generally, the double integral gives a signed volume. If $f$ is negative everywhere on $R,$ then the double integral of $f$ over $R$ is negative. If $f$ is both positive and negative on $R,$ then the double integral gives the difference between the positive and negative unsigned volumes. This is similar to the case with definite integrals for single-valued functions.\n\n- Finally, It's possible to use this definition with functions that are not continuous. If a function $f$ (not necessarily continuous) is defined over $R,$ we say that $f$ is **Riemann integrable** over $R$ if a unique number $I$ can be found that satisfies the above inequality. Many (though not all) non-continuous functions are Riemann integrable." :tutorial/id #uuid "b0889acb-60da-5e9c-9f1b-530b118b05f9" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5824 :tutorial/title "Final Remarks"} :step/id #uuid "3c30b46b-78e4-5a7f-bec5-89ef0019d000" :step/math-academy-id 35700}] :activity/title "Defining Double Integrals Using Lower and Upper Riemann Sums" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186129337}]}
